"The groES and groEL heat shock gene products of Escherichia coli are essential for bacterial growth at all temperatures - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC209756/?page=2)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; 1989 Mar;171(3):1379–1385. doi: 10.1128/jb.171.3.1379-1385.1989# The groES and groEL heat shock gene products of Escherichia coli are essential for bacterial growth at all temperatures.O Fayet ... The products of the groES and groEL genes of Escherichia coli, constituting the groE operon, are known to be required for growth at high temperature (42 degrees C) and are members of the heat shock regulon.\n\n. 1989 Mar;171(3):1379–1385. doi: 10.1128/jb.171.3.1379-1385.1989\n\n# The groES and groEL heat shock gene products of Escherichia coli are essential for bacterial growth at all temperatures.\n\nO Fayet\n\n### O Fayet\n\n^{1}Centre de Recherche de Biochimie et Genetique Cellulaires, Centre National de Recherche Scientifique, Toulouse, France.\n\nFind articles by O Fayet\n\n^{1}, T Ziegelhoffer\n\n### T Ziegelhoffer\n\n^{1}Centre de Recherche de Biochimie et Genetique Cellulaires, Centre National de Recherche Scientifique, Toulouse, France.\n\nFind articles by T Ziegelhoffer\n\n^{1}, C Georgopoulos\n\n### C Georgopoulos\n\n^{1}Centre de Recherche de Biochimie et Genetique Cellulaires, Centre National de Recherche Scientifique, Toulouse, France.\n\nFind articles by C Georgopoulos\n\n^{1}\n\n  * [Button: Author information]\n  * [Button: Copyright and License information]\n\n^{1}Centre de Recherche de Biochimie et Genetique Cellulaires, Centre National de Recherche Scientifique, Toulouse, France.\n\nPMC Copyright notice\n\nPMCID: PMC209756  PMID: 2563997\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe products of the groES and groEL genes of Escherichia coli, constituting the groE operon, are known to be required for growth at high temperature (42 degrees C) and are members of the heat shock regulon. Using a genetic approach, we examined the requirement for these gene products for bacterial growth at low temperature (17 to 30 degrees C). To do this, we constructed various groES groEL heterodiploid derivative strains. By inactivating one of the groE operons by a polar insertion, it was shown by bacteriophage P1 transduction that at least one of the groE genes was essential for growth at low temperature. Further P1 transduction experiments with strains that were heterodiploid for only one of the groE genes demonstrated that both groE gene products were required for growth at low temperature, which suggested a fundamental role for the groE proteins in E. coli growth and physiology.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGroES - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics (https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/agricultural-and-biological-sciences/groes)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Bacterial GroE, one of the best characterized molecular chaperone machines, is essential at all temperatures in E. coli (Fayet, Ziegelhoffer, & Georgopoulos, 1989). ... A different mechanism of GroEL/GroES assisted folding has been described for large proteins with molecular mass > 60 kDa.\n## Molecular Chaperones in Human Disorders\n\n2019, Advances in Protein Chemistry and Structural BiologyVinay Dahiya, Johannes Buchner\n\n### 2.1 The GroE molecular chaperone system\n\nBacterial GroE, one of the best characterized molecular chaperone machines, is essential at all temperatures in E. coli (Fayet, Ziegelhoffer, & Georgopoulos, 1989). GroE consists of two components: GroEL and GroES. GroEL is a double ring complex consisting of two times seven subunits. It was first identified in the context of T4 phage infection. Genetic defects in the GroE operon affected the ability of phages to assemble and compromised their infectivity (Georgopoulos, Hendrix, Kaiser, & Wood, 1972; Takano & Kakefuda, 1972). Later it was shown that RuBisCo, an enzyme responsible for the carbon fixation in cells, required a homolog of GroEL for the correct assembly of its large and small subunits in chloroplasts (Barraclough & Ellis, 1980; Goloubinoff, Christeller, Gatenby, & Lorimer, 1989; Hemmingsen et al., 1988). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDissertation zur Erlangung des Doktorgrades (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=413bd76bbf6b332c6af5cedd22a9372ca352f3a1&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; that the two proteins encoded in this operon, GroES and GroEL, are essential for theviability of E. coli at all temperatures (Fayet et al., 1989).\nSummary \n22 \nsensitive mutations in the GroE operon were unable to support the growth of bacteriophage \nλ (Georgopoulos et al., 1973). It was subsequently demonstrated by Georgopoulos’s group \nthat the two proteins encoded in this operon, GroES and GroEL, are essential for the \nviability of E. coli at all temperatures (Fayet et al., 1989). In 1980s, John Ellis and \ncolleagues identified a 60 kDa RuBisCo binding protein (RBP) that transiently associated \nwith chloroplast RuBisCo Large-subunits during synthesis (Barraclough and Ellis, 1980) \nand RBP was then recognized as a homolog of GroEL. The in vitro refolding efficiency of \nbacterial RuBisCo was shown to be significantly improved by GroEL and GroES \n(Goloubinoff et al., 1989). Along with these findings, mitochondrial hsp60 was soon found \nto play a role in the folding of proteins imported into mitochondria (Ostermann et al., 1989).  \nGroEL contains 14 identical 57-kDa subunits arranged in two stacked rings of seven \nsubunits each. And according to its crystal structure, each subunit of GroEL consists of three \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSubstoichiometric amounts of the molecular chaperones GroEL and GroES prevent thermal denaturation and aggregation of mammalian mitochondrial malate dehydrogenase in vitro 